Mild productive cough yielding clear sputum in an afebrile and systemically well patient
Minor Ailments and Upsets
Clear mucoid sputum, Afebrile state, Normal pulmonary auscultation

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MAU-020
Reason for Visit: Persistent productive cough causing significant anxiety regarding pneumonia or lower respiratory tract infection.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted a persistent productive cough following a viral illness. Urgent clinical assessment was necessary prior to the next in-hours period to auscultate the lungs, assess work of breathing, and definitively rule out early community-acquired pneumonia or exacerbation of reactive airways disease, ensuring respiratory stability.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I can't stop coughing and bringing up clear phlegm, and my chest aches from the effort.
History of Present Illness: Patient describes an intermittent cough producing scant clear or white mucoid sputum for the past 7 days. Reports a history of a recent mild viral upper respiratory infection that has otherwise resolved. Notes a mild substernal ache directly related to the mechanical force of coughing. Complete absence of pyrexia, shortness of breath, or wheezing. Lack of night sweats or unintended weight loss. Intact gag reflex and normal swallowing mechanism.
Functional Status: Fully independent, sleep slightly disturbed by coughing.
Social/Safety Concerns: Anxiety regarding potential severe lung infection.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 128/78 | HR: 76 | Temp: 36.6 C | RR: 14 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 79 Kg
General: Alert, well-appearing, coughing intermittently during conversation.
Physical Exam: Respiratory examination reveals a normal respiratory rate and effort. Clear bilateral lung fields on auscultation without crackles, rhonchi, or wheezes. Diagnostic triad confirmed: clear mucoid sputum, afebrile state, and normal pulmonary auscultation. Normal baseline oxygen saturation on room air. Chest wall is mildly tender to palpation diffusely, consistent with muscular strain from coughing. No signs of respiratory distress.
Environmental Observations: No obvious indoor respiratory irritants.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Patient taking over-the-counter dextromethorphan with partial relief.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Mild productive cough yielding clear sputum in an afebrile and systemically well patient (Acute Bronchitis) - Status: Improving
Differentials:

Community-Acquired Pneumonia - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Complete absence of fever, clear lung fields without focal crackles or consolidation signs, and normal oxygen saturations.

Asthma Exacerbation - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of wheezing, normal respiratory effort, and lack of chest tightness beyond mechanical strain.

Pulmonary Embolism - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Normal heart rate, absence of pleuritic chest pain, no hemoptysis, and no shortness of breath at rest.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient's persistent productive cough raised concern for lower respiratory tract pathology, specifically early pneumonia. Delaying physical examination until the regular clinic hours posed a risk of missing signs of hypoxia or focal consolidation that would necessitate prompt antibiotic therapy or radiographic imaging. An urgent after-hours lung auscultation safely confirmed an uncomplicated post-viral acute bronchitis with normal oxygenation, successfully preventing inappropriate antibiotic prescribing and avoiding unnecessary ED presentation.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Reassurance regarding the benign, self-limiting nature of post-viral cough. Advice on maintaining hydration to thin secretions. Recommend oral honey (if >1 year old) for cough suppression at night.
Positive Behavioral Change: Drink a warm, non-caffeinated beverage (like honey and lemon tea) immediately before bed to soothe the inflamed mucosal lining and mechanically thin secretions, reducing nocturnal cough frequency.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None indicated.
DME/Safety (Optional): None indicated.
Follow-up: Routine GP review if the cough persists for more than 3 weeks or changes in nature.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate presentation to Emergency Department or call 000 if you experience sudden shortness of breath, severe sharp chest pain when breathing in, or if you begin coughing up large amounts of blood.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is clinical post-viral airway inflammation; acute bronchitis is predominantly viral and does not respond to antibiotics.
Clinical Course: A post-viral cough typically lasts 2 to 3 weeks as the airway lining repairs itself from the viral damage.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate medical attention if you develop a high fever, if the phlegm becomes dark green or rust-colored, or if you feel breathless while resting.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The virus damaged the microscopic hairs in your windpipe. Until they regrow, your body uses mucus and coughing to keep the lungs clean. The chest ache is just tired muscles from coughing.
Actionable Steps: Stay well hydrated to keep the mucus thin. Take paracetamol for the chest muscle ache. Do not ask for antibiotics, as they will not cure a viral cough.

